### Current Bath Context
The next experiment uses **PolarClean 10 wt% NIPS bath** — a partially miscible bath that slows phase separation relative to pure water, generally yielding softer, more open structures compared to the 0 wt% bath at equivalent conditions.

### Pareto Front Assessment (both objectives measured, quality acceptable)

Reviewing all experiments with valid Elastic Modulus and Pore Fraction measurements, the approximate non-dominated front (maximizing both) includes:

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Bath | Quality |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 14-0add-8deg-N2-1250s (run1, 0wt) | 103.6 | 0.75 | 0% | minor creases |
| 14-0add-8deg-N2-1250s (run2, 0wt) | 189.4 | 0.71 | 0% | clean |
| 14-0add-8deg-N2-1250s (run3, 0wt) | 171.5 | 0.63 | 0% | top gap |
| 14-0add-8deg-N2-1250s (run4, 0wt) | 246.0 | 0.57 | 0% | clean |
| 14p8-17deg-N2-1425s (run2, 0wt) | 217.0 | 0.82 | 0% | wrinkles |
| 15-9deg-N2-1600s (run1, 0wt) | 246.6 | 0.77 | 0% | clean |
| 15-9deg-N2-1600s (run4, 0wt) | 221.3 | 0.75 | 0% | top gap |
| 15-9deg-N2-1600s (run5, 0wt) | 276.8 | 0.85 | 0% | top gap |
| 15-9deg-N2-1600s (run8, 0wt) | 211.1 | 0.68 | 0% | clean |
| 15p5-6deg-10wt (run2) | 162.8 | 0.73 | 10% | clean |
| 15p5-6deg-10wt (run4) | 151.1 | 0.82 | 10% | clean |
| 14p5-10deg-10wt (run2) | 105.5 | 0.66 | 10% | clean |

**Key observations:**
- The **15-0add-9deg-N2-1600s** cluster (0wt bath) shows the most promising high-pore-fraction results (0.75–0.85) with moderate-to-good modulus (200–280 bar), but many runs have coverage defects and high variability. Run 1 (clean, 246 bar, 0.77) and run 8 (clean, 211 bar, 0.68) are the best-quality specimens.
- The **15p5-6deg-10wt bath** cluster shows clean membranes with pore fraction 0.73–0.82 but lower modulus (110–215 bar). Run 4 (151 bar, 0.82) is a strong pore-fraction result with clean quality.
- The **14-0add-8deg-N2-1250s** cluster (0wt bath) shows pore fraction 0.57–0.75 with modulus 103–246 bar; run 2 (189 bar, 0.71) is the best clean specimen.

**The 15-0add-9deg-N2-1600s condition** (polymer_wt=15, bath_temp=9, pullcast_speed=6, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=250, nips_bath_wait_time=1600, mixing_temp=40, N2=true) is a strong Pareto candidate with high pore fraction, but it has been run 10 times in the 0wt bath with highly variable results (pore fraction 0.50–0.85, modulus 198–315 bar, many coverage defects). **It has never been run in the current 10wt PolarClean bath.**

The 10wt bath slows phase separation, which typically reduces modulus but can increase pore fraction by allowing more solvent exchange before gelation. The 15p5-6deg-10wt results show this effect. However, we haven't tested the 15wt/9°C/250s condition in the 10wt bath — this is an unexplored but physically motivated combination.

### Validation vs. Exploration Decision

The 15-9deg-N2-1600s condition has been run many times in the 0wt bath but **never in the current 10wt bath**. The 15p5-6deg-10wt bath results are promising but at slightly different polymer concentration. 

Rather than repeating an already heavily-sampled 0wt condition, the most informative next step is to **explore the 15wt/9°C/250s parameters in the current 10wt bath** — this directly tests whether the high-pore-fraction behavior of this condition transfers to the new bath context, and whether the 10wt bath can simultaneously maintain or improve modulus (by slowing phase separation differently than at 5–6°C).

This is a genuine new point: polymer_wt=15.0, bath_temp=9, pullcast_speed=6, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=250, nips_bath_wait_time=1600, mixing_temp=40, N2=true, in the 10wt PolarClean bath. It is distinct from all prior 10wt bath experiments (which used 15.5wt or 14.5wt at similar conditions) and directly extends the most promising 0wt-bath cluster into the new bath context.

**Expected benefit:** If the 10wt bath preserves the high pore fraction (0.65–0.77) seen in the 0wt bath at these conditions while potentially softening the modulus less than at 5–6°C bath temps, this could yield a Pareto-improving point. The 10wt bath at 9°C (vs. 6°C) is slightly warmer, which may help membrane seating/coverage quality. The experiment will also clarify whether the 10wt bath effect on pore fraction is consistent across polymer concentrations (15 vs. 15.5 wt%).

